= Bounded rationality =

== Overview ==
'''Bounded rationality''' describes rational behavior under limits on information, computation, time, and other resources. Herbert A. Simon introduced the framework as an alternative to models that assume globally optimizing agents.

== Core idea ==
A bounded agent cannot search every possible action, calculate every consequence, or maintain unlimited information. Decision procedures therefore have costs and must be evaluated partly relative to the resources and environments in which they operate.

Bounded rationality is broader than [[Cognitive bias]]. A heuristic can be a rational response to limited resources even when it sometimes produces systematic error.

== Satisficing ==
Simon used [[Satisficing]] to describe search that stops when an option meets an aspiration level rather than proving that it is globally optimal.

== Human and artificial systems ==
The framework can also describe artificial systems with finite context, compute, memory, search depth, or latency budgets. Similar resource constraints do not imply identical cognitive mechanisms.

== References ==
* Herbert A. Simon, “A Behavioral Model of Rational Choice”, ''Quarterly Journal of Economics'' 69 (1955): 99–118.
* Herbert A. Simon, ''Models of Man'' (1957).
* “Bounded Rationality”, ''Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy'', substantive revision 2024.

